1. Introduction
LiMe World (.lw) is the scene description format for LiMe, a Roblox-style game engine built on top of LMLua. A .lw file describes an entire world — its instances, their properties, their hierarchy, lighting, physics, and attached scripts — using JSON.

The format is intentionally:

JSON-based — no custom parsers, no XML, no binary blobs

Human-readable — openable in any text editor

Diff-friendly — version control works naturally

LMLua-aware — scripts embedded in .lw files are LMLua source

Roblox-inspired — the instance tree mirrors Roblox's Instance model, but simplified and modernized

1.1 File Extension
World files use the extension .lw.

1.2 MIME Type
application/x-lime-world+json

1.3 Hello, World
The smallest valid .lw file:

| {
| | "format": "lime-world",
| | "version": "1.1",
| | "instances": []
| }

2. File Structure
A .lw file is a single JSON object with the following top-level fields:

Field	Type	Required	Description
format	string	Yes	Must be "lime-world"
version	string	Yes	Format version, e.g. "1.1"
metadata	object	No	World metadata (name, author, etc.)
instances	array	Yes	Instances of the world tree
lighting	object	No	Global lighting settings
environment	object	No	Sky, fog, atmosphere
camera	object	No	Default camera configuration
spawn	object	No	Default spawn point
physics	object	No	Global physics settings
assets	object	No	Asset registry (meshes, textures, sounds)
All other top-level fields are ignored by the parser, which keeps the format forward-compatible.

3. The Root Object
Every .lw file begins with a root object carrying the format tag, version, optional metadata, the instances array, and any optional global sections.

The format field is required and must be the literal string "lime-world". Any other value causes the loader to reject the file.

The version field is required and carries a semantic version string. Version "1.1" provides:

All 26 instance classes

Generic properties resolved by instID

Dotted-path parenting

Implicit top-level containers

Lighting, camera, spawn, physics, environment

Inline LMLua scripts via sourceCode

4. Instances — Overview
An instance is any object in the world — a part, a model, a light, a script, a UI element, a player, a sound, or a network event.

4.1 Instance Fields
Field	Type	Required	Description
instID	string	Yes	Instance class identifier (e.g. "Part")
name	string	No	Instance name, defaults to instID
parent	string	No	Parent path (e.g. "workspace")
properties	object	No	Property map
tags	array	No	Collection tags (strings)
attributes	object	No	Custom user-defined attributes
id	string	No	Unique identifier
4.2 Minimal Instance
| {
| | "instID": "Part",
| | "name": "Floor",
| | "parent": "workspace"
| }

4.3 Full Instance
| {
| | "instID": "Part",
| | "name": "Floor",
| | "parent": "workspace",
| | "id": "part_floor_01",
| | "tags": ["static", "walkable"],
| | "attributes": {
| | | "CustomTag": "Lobby"
| | },
| | "properties": {
| | | "coordinates": [0, 0, 0],
| | | "size": [50, 1, 50],
| | | "color": [128, 128, 128],
| | | "surfaceMaterial": "Concrete",
| | | "anchored": true,
| | | "shape": "Box"
| | }
| }

4.4 The instID Field
instID is the authoritative class identifier. The system uses it to determine:

Which properties are valid

Which runtime behavior applies

Which type the instance resolves to in LMLua

There is no separate class field. instID is the class.

4.5 The properties Object
All visual, physical, and behavioral properties of an instance live inside properties. This keeps the top-level fields (instID, name, parent) clean and readable, and groups all mutable state in one place.

Property names are generic wherever possible — the same name (color, size, text) is reused across classes, and the loader resolves meaning from instID.

Example — a Part with size of vec3:

| {
| | "instID": "Part",
| | "properties": {
| | | "size": [50, 1, 50]
| | }
| }

Example — a Frame with size of dims:

| {
| | "instID": "Frame",
| | "properties": {
| | | "size": [200, 100]
| | }
| }

Same name, different type, resolved by class.

5. Instance Classes — Overview
LiMe World defines 26 built-in instance classes, organized into seven categories:

5.1 3D Physical & Environmental (9)
Part, SpawnLocation, Model, PointLight, RigJoint, Rig, Attachment, Camera, Folder

5.2 Networking & Backend Code (3)
RemoteEvent, BindableEvent, Script

5.3 User Interface (8)
ScreenGui, Frame, ScrollFrame, TextLabel, TextButton, ImageLabel, ImageButton, TextBox

5.4 Client-Side Code (1)
LocalScript

5.5 Player / Character / Team (3)
Player, Character, Team

5.6 Items (1)
Gear

5.7 Audio (1)
Sound

6. 3D Physical & Environmental Instances
6.1 Part
Description: The basic 3D building block used to create floors, walls, pillars, and physical structures.

Properties:

Property	Type	Description
coordinates	vec3	The 3D position of the part
size	vec3	Width, height, and depth
color	color	RGB color (0–255)
surfaceMaterial	str	Visual material style (e.g. "Slate")
anchored	boolean	Whether the part is anchored in place
shape	str	Geometry shape: "Box", "Ball", "Cylinder", "Wedge"
Example:

| {
| | "instID": "Part",
| | "name": "Wall",
| | "parent": "workspace",
| | "properties": {
| | | "coordinates": [10, 5, 0],
| | | "size": [1, 10, 20],
| | | "color": [200, 200, 200],
| | | "surfaceMaterial": "Brick",
| | | "anchored": true,
| | | "shape": "Box"
| | }
| }

6.2 SpawnLocation
Description: A specialized part where players spawn or respawn into the game world.

Properties:

Property	Type	Description
coordinates	vec3	The 3D position
size	vec3	Dimensions
color	color	RGB color
anchored	boolean	Whether the spawn is anchored
shape	str	Geometry shape
Example:

| {
| | "instID": "SpawnLocation",
| | "name": "RedSpawn",
| | "parent": "workspace",
| | "properties": {
| | | "coordinates": [0, 5, 0],
| | | "size": [6, 1, 6],
| | | "color": [255, 0, 0],
| | | "anchored": true,
| | | "shape": "Box"
| | }
| }

6.3 Model
Description: A container used to group multiple 3D parts into a single organized object or collection.

Properties: (none required — a Model is purely a container)

Parts belonging to a model use a parent path that includes the model's name:

| {
| | "instID": "Part",
| | "name": "Door",
| | "parent": "workspace.House",
| | "properties": {
| | | "coordinates": [5, 2, 0],
| | | "size": [1, 4, 2]
| | }
| }

6.4 PointLight
Description: A lighting object that casts light in all directions from a single point, with adjustable brightness, range, and color.

Properties:

Property	Type	Description
brightness	int / number	How intense the light output is
range	int / number	Maximum distance the light reaches
color	color	RGB tint of the light
Example:

| {
| | "instID": "PointLight",
| | "name": "CeilingLamp",
| | "parent": "workspace.House",
| | "properties": {
| | | "brightness": 5,
| | | "range": 30,
| | | "color": [255, 240, 200]
| | }
| }

6.5 RigJoint
Description: A Motor6D-style joint that connects two parts using a rotation and position transform. Used to build skeletons for characters and NPCs.

Properties:

Property	Type	Description
coordinates	vec3	The joint transform (position + rotation)
part0	str	Name or path of the first joined part
part1	str	Name or path of the second joined part
Example:

| {
| | "instID": "RigJoint",
| | "name": "LeftShoulder",
| | "parent": "workspace.NPC",
| | "properties": {
| | | "coordinates": [0, 0, 0],
| | | "part0": "Torso",
| | | "part1": "LeftArm"
| | }
| }

6.6 Rig
Description: A Humanoid-style controller that drives a model built from parts connected by RigJoints. Manages health, movement, and state. Animations are not supported in this version.

Properties:

Property	Type	Description
Health	number	Current health
MaxHealth	number	Maximum health
WalkSpeed	number	Movement speed
JumpPower	number	Jump strength
State	str	Current state ("Idle", "Walking", "Jumping", "Falling")
RootPart	str	Name of the root part
HeadPart	str	Name of the head part
TorsoPart	str	Name of the torso part
Example:

| {
| | "instID": "Rig",
| | "name": "PlayerRig",
| | "parent": "workspace.Player1",
| | "properties": {
| | | "Health": 100,
| | | "MaxHealth": 100,
| | | "WalkSpeed": 16,
| | | "JumpPower": 50,
| | | "State": "Idle",
| | | "RootPart": "HumanoidRootPart",
| | | "HeadPart": "Head",
| | | "TorsoPart": "Torso"
| | }
| }

Note: Rig properties are PascalCase because they are subproperties — see §14.

6.7 Attachment
Description: A transform anchor point that can be used by RigJoint, PointLight, or other transform-driven instances.

Properties:

Property	Type	Description
coordinates	vec3	The attachment position relative to its parent
Example:

| {
| | "instID": "Attachment",
| | "name": "MuzzlePoint",
| | "parent": "workspace.Pistol",
| | "properties": {
| | | "coordinates": [0, 0, -1.5]
| | }
| }

6.8 Camera
Description: A camera instance. The default camera is configured by the top-level camera section, but Camera instances may exist in the tree for scriptable cameras.

Properties: (reserved for future runtime API)

6.9 Folder
Description: A pure grouping container. Has no behavior of its own. Useful for organizing instances under a common parent.

Properties: (none)

7. Networking & Backend Code Instances
7.1 RemoteEvent
Description: A networking object that allows secure communication and data transfer between the server and clients.

Properties: (none — configured by name and parent)

Methods (runtime, in LMLua):

event:Connect(fn) — subscribe to incoming events

event:FireServer(...) — client to server

event:FireClient(player, ...) — server to one client

event:Fire(...) — local fire

Example:

| {
| | "instID": "RemoteEvent",
| | "name": "DamageEvent",
| | "parent": "replicated"
| }

7.2 BindableEvent
Description: An internal-only signal. Like RemoteEvent but never crosses the network — used for server-internal or client-internal signaling.

Properties: (none)

Methods (runtime, in LMLua):

event:Connect(fn)

event:Fire(...)

Example:

| {
| | "instID": "BindableEvent",
| | "name": "RoundStarted",
| | "parent": "server.storage"
| }

7.3 Script
Description: A backend server script that executes game logic, rules, and data management securely on the server.

Properties:

Property	Type	Description
sourceCode	str	The executable LMLua source code
Note: sourceCode is a multiline string — newlines must be encoded as \n inside the JSON string.

Placement: Script may be placed anywhere in the tree. Execution context is determined by the class, not the location. Scripts run in tree order (see §15).

Example:

| {
| | "instID": "Script",
| | "name": "GameLoop",
| | "parent": "server.scripts",
| | "properties": {
| | | "sourceCode": "server function onStart()\n output("Server started")\nend"
| | }
| }

8. User Interface (UI) Instances
8.1 ScreenGui
Description: The root container for 2D user interface elements rendered on the player's screen.

Properties: (none required)

Example:

| {
| | "instID": "ScreenGui",
| | "name": "MainGui",
| | "parent": "interface"
| }

8.2 Frame
Description: A 2D rectangular container used to group, align, and style UI elements.

Properties:

Property	Type	Description
position	dims	2D screen coordinates [X, Y]
size	dims	Pixel dimensions [Width, Height]
color	color	RGB background color
Example:

| {
| | "instID": "Frame",
| | "name": "HudPanel",
| | "parent": "interface.MainGui",
| | "properties": {
| | | "position": [10, 10],
| | | "size": [200, 100],
| | | "color": [30, 30, 30]
| | }
| }

8.3 ScrollFrame
Description: A Frame variant that allows scrolling its contents.

Properties: (all Frame properties, plus)

Property	Type	Description
canvasSize	dims	Total scrollable area size
Example:

| {
| | "instID": "ScrollFrame",
| | "name": "InventoryList",
| | "parent": "interface.MainGui",
| | "properties": {
| | | "position": [10, 120],
| | | "size": [200, 300],
| | | "color": [20, 20, 20],
| | | "canvasSize": [200, 800]
| | }
| }

8.4 TextLabel
Description: A UI component used to display static or dynamic text on screen.

Properties:

Property	Type	Description
position	dims	2D coordinates
size	dims	Dimensions
text	str	The text message displayed
color	color	Text color
Example:

| {
| | "instID": "TextLabel",
| | "name": "ScoreLabel",
| | "parent": "interface.MainGui.HudPanel",
| | "properties": {
| | | "position": [10, 10],
| | | "size": [180, 30],
| | | "text": "Score: 0",
| | | "color": [255, 255, 255]
| | }
| }

8.5 TextButton
Description: A clickable button UI element that players can interact with.

Properties:

Property	Type	Description
position	dims	2D coordinates
size	dims	Dimensions
text	str	The button label
color	color	Background color
Signals (runtime):

button.Click:Connect(fn) — fires when clicked

Example:

| {
| | "instID": "TextButton",
| | "name": "StartButton",
| | "parent": "interface.MainGui",
| | "properties": {
| | | "position": [200, 200],
| | | "size": [200, 60],
| | | "text": "Start Game",
| | | "color": [0, 120, 215]
| | }
| }

8.6 ImageLabel
Description: A UI component that displays an image.

Properties:

Property	Type	Description
position	dims	2D coordinates
size	dims	Dimensions
imageId	str	Asset path or URL
imageColor	color	Tint applied to the image
Example:

| {
| | "instID": "ImageLabel",
| | "name": "Logo",
| | "parent": "interface.MainGui",
| | "properties": {
| | | "position": [20, 20],
| | | "size": [128, 128],
| | | "imageId": "assets://logos/lime.png",
| | | "imageColor": [255, 255, 255]
| | }
| }

8.7 ImageButton
Description: A clickable UI element displaying an image.

Properties:

Property	Type	Description
position	dims	2D coordinates
size	dims	Dimensions
imageId	str	Asset path or URL
imageColor	color	Tint applied to the image
Signals (runtime):

button.Click:Connect(fn)

Example:

| {
| | "instID": "ImageButton",
| | "name": "SettingsButton",
| | "parent": "interface.MainGui",
| | "properties": {
| | | "position": [900, 20],
| | | "size": [48, 48],
| | | "imageId": "assets://icons/settings.png",
| | | "imageColor": [255, 255, 255]
| | }
| }

8.8 TextBox
Description: A text input field. Players can type into it.

Properties:

Property	Type	Description
position	dims	2D coordinates
size	dims	Dimensions
text	str	Current input text
placeholderText	str	Shown when empty
color	color	Background color
Signals (runtime):

box.FocusLost:Connect(fn) — fires when input focus is lost

Example:

| {
| | "instID": "TextBox",
| | "name": "ChatInput",
| | "parent": "interface.MainGui",
| | "properties": {
| | | "position": [10, 600],
| | | "size": [400, 30],
| | | "text": "",
| | | "placeholderText": "Type a message...",
| | | "color": [40, 40, 40]
| | }
| }

9. Client-Side Code Instances
9.1 LocalScript
Description: A client-side script that runs locally on the player's device to handle UI interactions and user inputs.

Properties:

Property	Type	Description
sourceCode	str	The executable LMLua source code
Placement: LocalScript may be placed anywhere in the tree, same as Script. Execution context is determined by the class.

Example:

| {
| | "instID": "LocalScript",
| | "name": "HudController",
| | "parent": "starter.player",
| | "properties": {
| | | "sourceCode": "client function onStart()\n output("Client ready")\nend"
| | }
| }

10. Player / Character / Team Instances
10.1 Player
Description: A player object representing a connected user.

Properties:

Property	Type	Description
userId	str	Unique user identifier
displayName	str	Display name
team	str	Team name, if any
Runtime (LMLua):

player.Character — the player's character instance

player:LoadData() / player:SaveData() — Supabase-backed (design-only)

Example:

| {
| | "instID": "Player",
| | "name": "Player1",
| | "parent": "starter.player",
| | "properties": {
| | | "userId": "0",
| | | "displayName": "Guest",
| | | "team": "Neutral"
| | }
| }

10.2 Character
Description: A player's or NPC's body. Contains parts joined by RigJoints and a Rig controller.

Properties: (none required — structure is defined by children)

Example:

| {
| | "instID": "Character",
| | "name": "PlayerCharacter",
| | "parent": "starter.character"
| }

10.3 Team
Description: A team assignment container. Players may belong to a team.

Properties:

Property	Type	Description
color	color	Team color
autoAssign	boolean	Whether new players are auto-assigned
Example:

| {
| | "instID": "Team",
| | "name": "Red",
| | "parent": "server.storage",
| | "properties": {
| | | "color": [255, 0, 0],
| | | "autoAssign": false
| | }
| }

11. Items & Audio Instances
11.1 Gear
Description: An item that can be held by a character — a sword, gun, tool, or any equippable object.

Properties:

Property	Type	Description
canBeDropped	boolean	Whether the gear can be dropped
requiresHold	boolean	Whether the character must hold it to use it
Example:

| {
| | "instID": "Gear",
| | "name": "Sword",
| | "parent": "starter.gears",
| | "properties": {
| | | "canBeDropped": true,
| | | "requiresHold": true
| | }
| }

11.2 Sound
Description: An audio source that can be parented to a part, model, or UI element.

Properties:

Property	Type	Description
soundId	str	Asset path or URL
volume	number	Playback volume (0.0–1.0)
pitch	number	Playback pitch multiplier
loop	boolean	Whether the sound loops
