range	number	Audible distance (when is3D is true)
is3D	boolean	Whether the sound is positional
Example:

| {
| | "instID": "Sound",
| | "name": "Footstep",
| | "parent": "workspace.Player1.LeftFoot",
| | "properties": {
| | | "soundId": "assets://sounds/step_grass.ogg",
| | | "volume": 0.6,
| | | "pitch": 1.0,
| | | "loop": false,
| | | "playing": false,
| | | "range": 20,
| | | "is3D": true
| | }
| }

12. Property Types
LiMe World defines a small set of JSON-encodable types. Every type is represented as a JSON array or a plain scalar.

12.1 Scalars
Type	JSON form	Example
str	JSON string	"Concrete"
int	JSON integer	42
number	JSON number	3.14
boolean	JSON bool	true
null	JSON null	null
12.2 Composites
Type	JSON form	Example
vec3	three numbers [X, Y, Z]	[10, 5, -3]
dims	two numbers [Width, Height]	[200, 100]
color	three integers [R, G, B] (0–255)	[255, 128, 0]
12.3 Property Name Resolution
Property names are generic wherever possible. The loader resolves meaning from instID.

Example — size is vec3 on Part, dims on Frame:

instID	Property	Resolved type
Part	size	vec3
Frame	size	dims
Part	coordinates	vec3
Frame	position	dims
Class-specific properties (surfaceMaterial on Part, soundId on Sound) keep their unique names because there is nothing to collide with.

12.4 Position vs. Coordinates
3D instances use coordinates (vec3)

UI instances use position (dims)

size is used by both (vec3 for 3D, dims for UI)

No instance has both coordinates and position.

13. Parenting & Hierarchy
LiMe World uses dotted paths for parenting. Every instance declares its parent as a dot-separated path, where the first segment is a top-level container and each further segment names a nested instance.

13.1 Top-Level Containers
The following top-level containers are implicit — they always exist and are never declared in the instances array:

Container	Purpose
workspace	The 3D scene container
replicated	Replicated storage
server	Server container
interface	UI container
starter	Player template container
13.2 Sub-Containers
The following sub-containers also exist implicitly:

Container	Path	Purpose
Storage	server.storage	Server-side storage
Scripts	server.scripts	Server scripts
Character	starter.character	Character template
Player	starter.player	Player template
Gears	starter.gears	Gear template
13.3 Path Examples
Path	Meaning
workspace	The 3D scene container
workspace.House	A Model named House inside the workspace
workspace.House.Door	A Part named Door inside House
replicated	The Replicated container
server	The Server container
server.storage	Server-side storage
server.scripts	Server scripts
interface	The UI container
interface.MainGui	A ScreenGui named MainGui
interface.MainGui.HudPanel	A Frame inside MainGui
starter	The player template container
starter.character	Character template
starter.player	Player template
starter.gears	Gear template
13.4 Rules
Every instance's parent must reference an existing container or instance.

Top-level containers (workspace, replicated, server, interface, starter) are implicit and never declared.

A path segment matches an instance's name.

. is the only valid separator.

The first segment is always a top-level container.

14. Naming Conventions
LiMe World uses two casing styles depending on the level:

14.1 camelCase — Main Properties
Used for:

Top-level instance fields: instID, name, parent, properties, tags, attributes, id

Main property names inside properties: coordinates, size, position, color, text, brightness, range, anchored, shape, surfaceMaterial, sourceCode, soundId, imageId, imageColor, volume, pitch, loop, playing, is3D, placeholderText, canvasSize, userId, displayName, team, canBeDropped, requiresHold, part0, part1

Top-level container names: workspace, replicated, server, interface, starter

Sub-container names: storage, scripts, character, player, gears

14.2 PascalCase — Subproperties and Values
Used for:

Nested properties inside properties (e.g. Rig.Health, Rig.MaxHealth, Rig.WalkSpeed, Rig.JumpPower, Rig.State, Rig.RootPart, Rig.HeadPart, Rig.TorsoPart)

Values of enum-like strings: "Box", "Ball", "Cylinder", "Wedge", "Slate", "Concrete", "Voxel", "Fixed", "Idle", "Walking", "Jumping", "Falling"

Content inside attributes, metadata, lighting, environment, camera, spawn, physics, assets

14.3 Class Names
Class names (instID values) are PascalCase:

Part, SpawnLocation, Model, PointLight, RigJoint, Rig, Attachment, Camera, Folder, RemoteEvent, BindableEvent, Script, ScreenGui, Frame, ScrollFrame, TextLabel, TextButton, ImageLabel, ImageButton, TextBox, LocalScript, Player, Character, Team, Gear, Sound

14.4 Why the Split?
Top-level and main properties are written frequently by hand, so camelCase keeps .lw files compact and readable. Subproperties and enum values are less frequent, and PascalCase matches the convention used by the LMLua runtime and by Roblox's Enum system.

15. Scripts & LMLua Integration
Scripts are instances with instID: "Script" (server) or instID: "LocalScript" (client). Their LMLua source lives in sourceCode as a JSON string with embedded \n newlines.

15.1 Placement
Script and LocalScript may be placed anywhere in the tree. There are no forbidden paths.

The class determines execution context, not the location:

Class	Runs on
Script	Server
LocalScript	Client
15.2 Execution Order
Scripts execute in tree order:

Parents before children

Siblings in array order

Across the entire instances array, depth-first

This is deterministic and matches how most engines load scenes.

15.3 Accessing Instances from LMLua
Scripts access instances through the game global using dotted paths:

| local floor = workspace.Floor
| local lamp = workspace.House.WallLamp
|
| floor.color = [255, 0, 0]
| lamp.brightness = 5

15.4 Signals
All event-bearing instances expose the same signal API:

Method	Meaning
:Connect(fn)	Subscribe to the event
:Fire(...)	Local fire
:FireServer(...)	Client to server (RemoteEvent only)
:FireClient(player, ...)	Server to one client (RemoteEvent only)
TextButton, ImageButton, and TextBox expose .Click and .FocusLost signals with the same :Connect method.

16. World Metadata
The metadata object describes the world itself. It is not part of the runtime scene graph, but is exposed to scripts via the game global.

Field	Type	Description
Name	string	World display name
Author	string	Author name
Description	string	Long description
Created	string	ISO 8601 timestamp
Modified	string	ISO 8601 timestamp
Tags	array	Keywords
Thumbnail	string	Path or URL to a preview image
Note: metadata uses PascalCase because it is a nested object — see §14.

Example:

| {
| | "format": "lime-world",
| | "version": "1.1",
| | "metadata": {
| | | "Name": "My First World",
| | | "Author": "Player1",
| | | "Description": "A small test world.",
